CI/CD stands for Continuous Integration and Continuous Delivery or Continuous Deployment. 
- An automated software development workflow that replaces slow, error-prone manual testing and releases with a smooth, reliable pipeline.

What is Continuous Integration (CI)?
Continuous Integration is the practice of merging code changes into a shared central repository (like GitHub or GitLab) frequently—often multiple times a day.
- Automatic Builds: Every time you push code or open a pull request, the CI system builds your application to make sure it compiles cleanly. 
- Automated Tests: It automatically runs unit tests, linting, and type-checking. 
- Early Bug Detection: If a test fails, the pipeline blocks the merge and alerts the developer immediately, catching bugs when they are small and easy to fix.

What is Continuous Delivery vs. Continuous Deployment (CD)?
The "CD" phase takes your tested code and prepares it or ships it to live environments. 
It comes in two forms: 
- Continuous Delivery: Code changes are automatically built, tested, and pushed to a staging environment that mimics production. However, a human must manually click a button to approve and release it to actual end users.
- Continuous Deployment: Code changes that pass all automated tests go live to production automatically with zero human intervention. 

